LIST
A list is an ordered, mutable collection of elements in Python.
•	Ordered: Elements maintain their positions.
•	Mutable: You can modify a list after creating it.
•	Collection: A list can contain multiple elements.
Important: Python indexing starts at 0, not 1.
Lists are mutable.
. Adding Elements to Lists
Python provides three important methods.
A. append()
Adds an element to the end.
B. insert()
Adds an element at a specified index.
C. extend()
Adds multiple elements from another iterable.
append() adds one object.
extend() adds elements individually from an iterable.
Removing Elements
Three methods are particularly useful.
remove()
Removes the first matching value.
pop()
Removes an element using its index and returns that element.
del
Deletes an element or a slice.
Method	Purpose
append()	Adds one element
extend()	Adds multiple elements
insert()	Inserts at an index
remove()	Removes a matching value
pop()	Removes and returns an element
clear()	Removes all elements
index()	Finds the first matching index
count()	Counts occurrences
sort()	Sorts the list in place
reverse()	Reverses the list in place
copy()	Creates a shallow copy
Function	Purpose
max()	Finds the largest element
min()	Finds the smallest element
sum()	Calculates the total
len()	Counts elements
sorted()	Returns a sorted list
